This week we focused on building the first physical prototype and testing colour detection. 
The 3D-printed enclosure was printed on Friday. Before printing, we had to make sure the electronics would fit inside, 
so we shared the Arduino's dimensions (68.6 mm × 53.4 mm) with Sophie, who modelled the enclosure and posted it in our Google Doc for feedback.
We first tested with a breadboard, but the colour sensor we're using now connects directly to the Arduino without one. 
That helped, because the enclosure is small and a breadboard wouldn't have fit. The final setup is just the Arduino and the colour sensor
inside the printed case.

Testing colour detection

On Monday, the team met at 4 pm outside the computation lab to put everything together and test it. there were two printed shapes:
a handheld scanner with a rounded, ridged form that you hold over a surface
a flat box with a small opening on top where the colour sensor sits
Both were connected to a laptop by USB so we could read the sensor's values from the Arduino in real time. 
To test, the team held the scanner over a phone screen showing solid colours, and checked whether the sensor's readings matched. 
Using a phone screen was useful because it gave us clean, consistent colours before trying it on the actual ceramic beads.

Ceramic surfaces are glossy and uneven, so they will probably be harder to read.
Terence was working on getting the sensor to recognize a consistent pattern for each colour, videos were recorded  of the tests for documentation.
Testing the colour sensor with the 3D-printed enclosure
Holding the scanner made me think about how the audience will interact with the final piece. 

The shape matters: it needs to feel natural to hold, and it has to sit at the right distance from the bead for the sensor to read it accurately.
